<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Like School! – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> I Like School!</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> I Like Math!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: text: Identify objects on the pages of picture books, using simple words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can identify familiar objects in picture books by saying simple words to a peer (e.g., 'game' or 'pencil') when asked or when another student shows interest.
Can use gestures or point to objects in picture books to help peers understand preferences (e.g., pointing to a book and saying 'book' to support a peer's understanding).
Can help a peer understand preferences by connecting words to objects in picture books (e.g., saying 'I like books.' while pointing to a book or 'This is math.' when pointing to numbers).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0e6ed;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying large picture flashcards of different school subjects (math, art, music, science, playground, English). Teacher asks, "What do you like?" and points to each card. Students chime in with single words or simple phrases they remember from previous lessons (e.g., "Math!", "Games!"). The teacher leads a quick 'I like...' song, pointing to the cards and singing, "I like math, yes I do! I like math, how about you?" (Teacher models enthusiastically, using gestures).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture book open to a page showing children playing a math game. "Look! A game! I like games!" The teacher points to the image and clearly pronounces '/g/' in 'games'. Then, the teacher turns to a page with numbers and shapes. "This is math. I like math!" The teacher traces the /l/ sound in 'like' with a finger motion. The teacher then pretends to explain to a stuffed animal: "Look! This is a game! I like games! Do you like games?" The teacher models the interaction for helping a 'peer' understand.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a book) "Is this a book?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a math game in the book) "Are these numbers for math?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "I Like School!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large picture book featuring various school activities and subjects, specifically highlighting 'math' (counting, shapes, games) and other subjects like 'art' (colorful drawings), 'music' (children singing), 'playground' (kids playing games). The book should have clear, simple illustrations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand simple words connected to objects and activities in picture books, focusing on 'math' and 'games'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows pages of the picture book. "Look! What is this?" (Teacher points to a math game). Students say "Game!" or "Math!".</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do you like art?" (Teacher points to art supplies). Students respond with "Yes!" or "No!".</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you find the music?" Students point to the picture of children singing.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /l/ in 'like' and /g/ in 'games' by repeating "I like games" and "I like math" and having students echo.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 7-9 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Subject + verb ('I like school.'), Present simple ('I like games.'), Yes/No questions ('Do you like it?', 'Yes.').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> teacher, art, playground, science, music, math, English, enjoy, choose, create, count, build, sing, share, look, write, color, interesting, funny, boring, exciting, happy, sad, favorite, colorful.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /l/ in 'like', /g/ in 'games'. Phonemic awareness: 'math' and 'games'.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Expressing likes and dislikes ('I like...', 'I don't like...'), Answering preference questions ('yes', 'no').</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. 'Show and Tell' with Flashcards (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher distributes small flashcards with images of school subjects (math games, colorful art, music notes, playground swings). Students hold up the card the teacher names. "Show me math!" Then, the teacher says, "I like math!" and students repeat, pointing to their card. The teacher then says, "I don't like music!" and students repeat, putting their music card down. Focus on clear pronunciation of /l/ and /g/.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Picture Book Preference (Semi-controlled):</b> Students work in small groups with a simple picture book related to school activities. Each student takes a turn pointing to an object or activity in the book and saying, "I like [object/activity, e.g., 'art', 'games']" or "I don't like [object/activity]". Other students listen and confirm by nodding or shaking their heads. The teacher circulates, providing support and correcting pronunciation.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Explanation (Mediation Focus):</b> In pairs, one student shows a page from their picture book to their partner. They point to an object (e.g., a math game) and say, "Look! This is a game. I like games." The partner then asks, "Do you like it?" The first student responds "Yes!" or "No." This practices helping a peer understand their preferences by connecting words to objects and asking/answering simple questions. The teacher encourages gestures and pointing.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "I Like School!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Guided peer explanation and sharing of the "Likes & Dislikes Chart" (21st Century Skills Project).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students retrieve their "Likes & Dislikes Chart" created during previous lessons. This chart has simple pictures/drawings of school subjects/activities (e.g., a drawing of numbers for 'math', a crayon for 'art', a ball for 'playground') under two columns: 'I Like' and 'I Don't Like'.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In pairs, Student A shows their chart to Student B. Student A points to an item under "I Like" (e.g., a picture of a math game) and says, "Look! I like math. I like games." Student B may ask, "Do you like it?" or just point and say, "Math!" Student A confirms, "Yes!"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, Student A points to an item under "I Don't Like" (e.g., a picture of a boring book) and says, "I don't like boring books."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The students take turns, mediating their preferences to their peer by pointing, using simple words, and responding to simple questions, effectively helping their peer understand the content of their chart. The teacher circulates, offering support and encouraging the use of target vocabulary and phrases.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Focus on fluency and collaborative interaction, with students using gestures and pointing to help convey meaning.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the peer explanation of the "Likes & Dislikes Chart".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "Likes & Dislikes Chart" to a peer, identifying objects and explaining preferences, and responding to their peer's simple questions or interest.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Identifies at least 2-3 items from the chart using target words (e.g., 'math', 'games', 'art').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Expression of preferences:</b> Uses 'I like...' or 'I don't like...' correctly.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mediation skills:</b> Points to objects in the chart to help the peer understand.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity & Intelligibility:</b> Words are generally clear and understandable (e.g., /l/ in 'like', /g/ in 'games' are distinguishable).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Attempts to interact and respond to the peer (e.g., answering 'Do you like it?').</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> The teacher shows two large pictures: a happy face and a neutral face. "Can you tell your friend what you like?" Students point to the happy face if they feel they can, or the neutral face if they need more help. Alternatively, they can simply say "Yes!" or "No!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 7-9 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you liked telling your friend about your chart?" (Teacher models: "I liked telling my friend 'I like games!'")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or a little hard to help your friend understand?" (Students can show thumbs up for easy, thumbs sideways for a little hard).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The peer mediation using the "Likes & Dislikes Chart" was effective in encouraging students to use target vocabulary and simple sentences to explain their preferences. The use of pointing and gestures was crucial for Kinder students to convey meaning. Some students still need more prompting for the 'Do you like it?' question and responding with 'Yes' or 'No'. I will continue to integrate more opportunities for simple question-answer exchanges and modeling peer-to-peer explanations with visual aids in future lessons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "I Like School!" theme by consolidating learning through a mediation activity. The next theme will introduce new vocabulary and concepts, building on the foundation of expressing preferences and engaging in simple peer interactions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> 3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>